<h1>Sessions &amp; contracts</h1>
<p><strong>Multiplayer</strong> means several machines run copies of the same game and exchange session state. A <strong>session</strong> carries connection identity, metadata such as scene and seed, and network entity ownership. A <strong>network contract</strong> declares which messages exist, who may send them, and what happens on disconnect. The runtime enforces these rules at its protocol boundary.</p>

<h2>What a NetworkContract asset is</h2>
<p>A <strong>NetworkContract</strong> is an asset declaring the multiplayer rules of a game. The project points at it from <code>demi.project.json</code>:</p>
<pre><code>"network_contract": "asset://network/arena_contract"</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>network_contract</code> — the <code>asset://</code> ID of the contract asset. Required only for networked games.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every peer computes a <strong>contract hash</strong> from the contract content. The hash is checked at session setup: peers running different rules cannot join the same session, so a mismatch is rejected before any gameplay message flows.</p>

<h2>Policy: who may do what</h2>
<p>The contract answers three policy questions in advance:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Allowed values</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>senders / writers</td><td><code>server</code>, <code>owner</code>, <code>all</code></td><td>Who may send a given message or write a given piece of state. Spawning and transfer are server-only, regardless of message policy.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>disconnect</td><td><code>despawn</code>, <code>return_to_server</code>, <code>transfer_by_game_policy</code></td><td>What happens to a player's entities when they leave: destroy them, hand control back to the server, or let game code decide.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>message targets</td><td><code>owned_entity</code>, <code>entity</code>, <code>none</code></td><td>Which entity a message may address: only an entity the sender owns, any entity, or no entity at all (session-level messages).</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Validation rejects messages that violate these declared rules before Lua sees them. Game-specific effects such as damage still need authoritative gameplay checks.</p>

<h2>Session phases</h2>
<p>A session moves through named <strong>phases</strong>. Inspect <code>diagnostics().phase</code> and <code>secure_ready</code> to observe protocol progress:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>closed</code></td><td>No session. The transport is idle.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>connected</code></td><td>A transport link exists; handshake not finished.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>authenticated</code></td><td>The contract protocol handshake has been accepted. This phase does not by itself provide encrypted transport or account authentication.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ready</code></td><td>Handshake progression immediately before protocol activation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>active</code></td><td>The contract protocol is active; game code still owns scene and simulation setup.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>reconnecting</code></td><td>A lifecycle phase defined by the protocol; automatic Lua session reconnect is not integrated.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The normal path is <code>closed → connected → authenticated → ready → active</code>. The host reaches <code>active</code> when hosting starts; the client reaches it after the accepted handshake. The current Lua session resets on client disconnect; it does not automatically resume a dropped connection.</p>

<h2>Hosting and connecting</h2>
<p>Import the session service explicitly and either host or connect:</p>
<pre><code>local Session = require("demi.network.session")

local ok = Session.host(7777)
if ok then
    Session.start_session({ scene_id = "scene://game/arena", seed = 42 })
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>require("demi.network.session")</code> — imports the session service table.</li>
  <li><code>Session.host(port)</code> — opens an ENet host on the given UDP port; configured certificates select DTLS. Returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>Session.start_session(metadata)</code> — stores and sends metadata such as <code>scene_id</code> and <code>seed</code> from the host. It returns nothing and does not itself load a scene or seed the simulation. See <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'simulation-and-seeds'}) }}">Simulation &amp; seeds</a>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>local ok = Session.connect("192.168.1.10", 7777)
print(Session.is_connected())   -- true once the transport link is up</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.connect(host, port)</code> — starts connecting a client to a host. Returns a boolean for "connection attempt started".</li>
  <li><code>Session.is_connected()</code> — returns <code>true</code> while a transport link exists, <code>false</code> otherwise.</li>
  <li><code>Session.is_host()</code> — returns <code>true</code> on the server peer, <code>false</code> on clients.</li>
  <li><code>Session.sender_id()</code> — returns this peer's string sender ID, assigned during the handshake on clients.</li>
  <li><code>Session.current_session()</code> — returns the current session table or <code>nil</code> when none is active.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Receiving validated messages</h2>
<p>Incoming traffic is checked <strong>before</strong> it reaches Lua: header, protocol version, contract hash, epoch, sequence, size, message structure, finite numbers, payload schema, sender identity, destination, ownership generation, and rate limits. Only messages that pass arrive in the event queue.</p>
<pre><code>function Game:on_update(dt)
    local update = Session.process_events()
    for _, msg in ipairs(update.events) do
        if msg.name == "player_join" then
            self:handle_join(msg.sender_id, msg.data)
        end
    end
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.process_events()</code> — drains network events and returns an update table with <code>connected</code>, <code>disconnected</code>, <code>session_started</code>, <code>session</code>, <code>messages</code>, and <code>events</code>. The events array includes validated game messages and prediction events.</li>
  <li><code>msg.name</code> — a declared game message name or a prediction event such as <code>prediction_corrected</code>. Dispatch only the event types your game handles.</li>
  <li><code>msg.sender_id</code> — string ID derived from the transport peer for game messages, rather than a sender ID supplied in the payload.</li>
  <li><code>msg.data</code> — the validated payload as a Lua table. Array-like payloads round-trip as numeric arrays.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tests and game code consume the same queues. Poll state in tests when gameplay legitimately drains connection events.</p>

<h2>Entities, IDs, and ownership</h2>
<p>The <strong>server</strong> issues string network IDs and ownership generations. A generation counter prevents stale operations from a previous owner from applying.</p>
<pre><code>local network_id = Session.spawn("player", entity_id, owner_id)
print(network_id)   -- string network ID or nil (host only)</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.spawn(prefab_key, entity_id, owner)</code> — host only. Uses a <code>replicated_prefabs</code> key and an existing local entity ID, then assigns an owner. Returns a string network ID or <code>nil</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>Session.transfer(network_id, new_owner_id)   -- move ownership
Session.despawn(network_id)                  -- remove the replicated entity</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.transfer</code> — reassigns ownership to <code>new_owner_id</code> and advances the ownership generation; returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>Session.despawn</code> — removes the replicated entity from the session; returns a boolean.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>if Session.has_authority(network_id) then
    -- this peer decides that entity's state
end
local owner = Session.owner(network_id)                       -- string owner ID or nil
local nid   = Session.network_id_for_owner(owner_id)          -- string network ID or nil
Session.bind_local_entity(network_id, local_entity_id)        -- boolean; owner only</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.has_authority(network_id)</code> — returns whether this peer owns the entity. Contract field writer rules still govern publication.</li>
  <li><code>Session.owner(network_id)</code> — returns the string owner ID, or <code>nil</code> for an unknown entity.</li>
  <li><code>Session.network_id_for_owner(owner_id)</code> — returns the first network ID that owner controls, or <code>nil</code>.</li>
  <li><code>Session.bind_local_entity(network_id, local_entity_id)</code> — on the owning peer, links a network ID to an existing local scene entity for publication; returns a boolean.</li>
</ul>
<p>Late joiners receive retained spawn state: every still-live replicated entity is re-spawned for them from the session's spawn record, so a new client sees the arena as it is, not as it was at match start.</p>

<h2>Sending messages</h2>
<pre><code>local ok = Session.send("player_join", nil, { color = { 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, 1.0 } })</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.send(name, target, data)</code> — queues a message declared in the contract.</li>
  <li><code>name</code> — the message name from the contract.</li>
  <li><code>target</code> — a string network entity ID when the contract's target rule requires one, or <code>nil</code> for a <code>none</code> target. The rule is declared in the contract, not passed as an option.</li>
  <li><code>data</code> — the payload. It must satisfy the contract's schema and finite-number and size limits.</li>
  <li>Returns a boolean for local validation and queueing. Remote delivery is subject to the receiver's checks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watching session health</h2>
<pre><code>local d = Session.diagnostics()
print(d.phase, d.session_epoch, d.contract_hash)
print(d.sent_messages, d.received_messages, d.rejected_messages)
print(d.secure_accepted_messages, d.secure_rejected_messages)</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.diagnostics()</code> — returns a table with <code>phase</code>, <code>session_epoch</code>, <code>contract_hash</code>, transport and security counters, <code>secure_ready</code>, and <code>last_error</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Counters grow whenever messages are accepted or rejected, so a rising rejection count is a policy or version mismatch worth investigating before it becomes a player complaint.</p>

<h2>Keeping time in sync</h2>
<p>Replicated entities advance through the session service at the simulation rate:</p>
<pre><code>function Game:on_fixed_update(fixed_dt)
    Session.update_entity(network_id, fixed_dt)
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Session.update_entity(network_id, dt)</code> — on the owning peer, captures contract-declared replicated fields from the bound local entity and publishes them when its send interval elapses. Returns a boolean. Remote presentation is updated by <code>process_events()</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Smoothing and rewinding remote entities are the job of <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'prediction'}) }}">Prediction</a>. TLS and custom transports are covered in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'http-and-tls'}) }}">HTTP &amp; TLS</a>.</p>
